<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Anton von Kolowrat-Liebsteinsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Count Kolowrat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Anton von Kolowrat-Liebsteinsky was a Bohemian noble and Austrian statesman who directed internal and financial affairs from 1826. Under the incapable Emperor Ferdinand I he sat with Metternich in the State Conference that governed the empire, and their rivalry helped paralyse government before 1848. He briefly served as the first minister-president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-anton-von-kolowrat-liebsteinsky.jpg" alt="Franz Anton von Kolowrat-Liebsteinsky" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Anton_von_Kolowrat-Liebsteinsky_(1778%E2%80%931861)_engraving_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
